<p id="S4.F3.sf8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.sf8.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">OneFormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.sf8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">(h)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf9" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<p id="S4.F3.sf9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.sf9.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">CAFuser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.sf9.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">(i)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf10" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<p id="S4.F3.sf10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.sf10.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">DGFusion (<span id="S4.F3.sf10.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ours</span>)</span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.sf10.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">(j)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative comparison on MUSES with visualization of the input modalities. Best viewed on a screen at full zoom.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also investigate our depth loss design from <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Losses ‣ III DGFusion ‣ DGFusion: Depth-Guided Sensor Fusionfor Robust Semantic Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a>, with results presented in <a href="#S4.T5" title="In IV-B Ablations ‣ IV Experiments ‣ DGFusion: Depth-Guided Sensor Fusionfor Robust Semantic Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>. The baseline (ID 1), which is trained only with a basic <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> loss as depth supervision, achieves moderate performance.
Introducing our smoothness terms <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{es}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>es</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{es}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{pes}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>pes</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{pes}}</annotation></semantics></math> (ID 2) improves PQ slightly by +0.14%, indicating their benefit in regularizing sparse lidar depth supervision.
Finally, combining both our smoothness terms and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-quantile filtering (ID 4) results in the best overall performance, further improving PQ by +0.93%. Our smoothness losses shine especially at the presence of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-filtering, as removing the former (from ID 4 to ID 3) reduces PQ by -0.87%.
These experiments confirm the importance of explicitly addressing sparse and noisy lidar returns through outlier filtering and smoothness constraints for more robust depth guidance and hence improved panoptic segmentation.</p>
</div>
