</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate the robustness of our approach, we investigate the sensitivity of our method to variations in the hyperparameters introduced by our work. As shown in <a href="#S4.T6" title="In IV-B Ablations ‣ IV Experiments ‣ DGFusion: Depth-Guided Sensor Fusionfor Robust Semantic Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>, we evaluate performance across a range of hyperparameter values by scaling each parameter by <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5\times</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times</annotation></semantics></math> relative to our default settings. All five introduced hyperparameters (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{L1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>L1</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{L1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{es}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>es</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{es}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{pes}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>pes</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{pes}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{depth}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>depth</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{depth}}</annotation></semantics></math>) exhibit stable performance on the validation set with <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 1.24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>1.24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 1.24</annotation></semantics></math>% variation in PQ across these scaling factors. This stability demonstrates that our method does not require tedious hyperparameter tuning for each new deployment scenario, as evidenced by our cross-dataset generalization where the same hyperparameter values achieve SOTA performance on both MUSES (real-world) and DeLiVER (synthetic) without any dataset-specific tuning.</p>
</div>
